### Version

Version: 4.0.3

### Context

[RFC 6265 HTTP State Management Mechanism, 5.3 Storage Model, Step 11](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6265#section-5.3) defines the tuple (name, domain, path) as the cookie identifier. 
However, the implementation of [Http1xServerResponse](https://github.com/eclipse-vertx/vert.x/blob/4.0.3/src/main/java/io/vertx/core/http/impl/Http1xServerResponse.java#L787) and [Http2ServerResponse](https://github.com/eclipse-vertx/vert.x/blob/4.0.3/src/main/java/io/vertx/core/http/impl/Http2ServerResponse.java#L716) use only the cookie name as identifier.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Set two cookies with the same name but different path in a response header with
```java
public void handle(RoutingContext ctx) {
  Cookie cookie = Cookie.cookie("SOME_NAME", "foo").setPath("/");
  Cookie sameCookieWithDifferentPath = Cookie.cookie("SOME_NAME", "bar").setPath("/somePath");

  ctx.response().addCookie(cookie);
  ctx.response().addCookie(sameCookieWithDifferentPath);
  ctx.response().end();
}
```
2. Expect 2 cookies on the client, but only get 1.

comments.body:
I would rather not do some string magic to create a key. Simply, because these values may already contains the meta character we would use.

I would rather:
* Create a `Tuple` object, wrapping name, domain and path, that we would use as a key i.e. `Map<Tuple, ServerCookie>`.  `Tuple` would be a new class implementing the `Comparable` interface, overwriting the `equals` method and maybe the `hashCode` method.
* Create a map of maps of maps i.e. `Map<String, Map<String, Map<String, ServerCookie>>>`. A cookie retrieval would then look like `ServerCookie cookie = cookies().get(name).get(domain).get(path)` (of course with some careful checks in between when there is no such value). Actually [guava](https://github.com/google/guava/wiki/NewCollectionTypesExplained#table) does provide this functionality.

However, I don't think we can circumvent breaking the public API.

_Addendum:_
For the sake of talking about the same topic, I consider the following as the public API regarding cookies:

[HttpClientResponse.java](https://github.com/eclipse-vertx/vert.x/blob/4.0.3/src/main/java/io/vertx/core/http/HttpClientResponse.java)
```java
  /**
   * @return the Set-Cookie headers (including trailers)
   */
  @CacheReturn
  List<String> cookies();
```

[HttpServerRequest.java](https://github.com/eclipse-vertx/vert.x/blob/4.0.3/src/main/java/io/vertx/core/http/HttpServerRequest.java)
```java
  /**
   * Get the cookie with the specified name.
   *
   * @param name  the cookie name
   * @return the cookie
   */
  default @Nullable Cookie getCookie(String name) {
    return cookieMap().get(name);
  }

  /**
   * @return the number of cookieMap.
   */
  default int cookieCount() {
    return cookieMap().size();
  }

  /**
   * @return a map of all the cookies.
   */
  Map<String, Cookie> cookieMap();
```

[HttpServerResponse.java](https://github.com/eclipse-vertx/vert.x/blob/4.0.3/src/main/java/io/vertx/core/http/HttpServerResponse.java)
```java
  /**
   * Add a cookie. This will be sent back to the client in the response.
   *
   * @param cookie  the cookie
   * @return a reference to this, so the API can be used fluently
   */
  @Fluent
  HttpServerResponse addCookie(Cookie cookie);


  /**
   * Expire a cookie, notifying a User Agent to remove it from its cookie jar.
   *
   * @param name  the name of the cookie
   * @return the cookie, if it existed, or null
   */
  default @Nullable Cookie removeCookie(String name) {
    return removeCookie(name, true);
  }

  /**
   * Remove a cookie from the cookie set. If invalidate is true then it will expire a cookie, notifying a User Agent to
   * remove it from its cookie jar.
   *
   * @param name  the name of the cookie
   * @return the cookie, if it existed, or null
   */
  @Nullable Cookie removeCookie(String name, boolean invalidate);
```

Just to reiterate what you said, to ensure I understood everything correctly:

You propose to change the public API as follows (only the changes shown from the public API shown above):

EDIT: removed javadocs to improve readabilty.

HttpClientResponse.java

```java
// nothing to change here
```

HttpServerRequest.java

```java
  @Nullable List<Cookie> getCookies(String name);
  MultiMap<String, Cookie> cookieMultiMap();
```

HttpServerResponse.java

```java
  List<Cookie> removeCookie(String name);
  List<Cookie> removeCookie(String name, boolean invalidate);

  @Nullable Cookie removeCookie(Cookie cookie);
  @Nullable Cookie removeCookie(Cookie cookie, boolean invalidate);
```

The breaking change in the code would then only be the return type of some methods (i.e. `List<Cookie>` instead of `Cookie` and `MultiMap<String, Cookie>` instead of `Map<Cookie>`). 

Another breaking change would be the semantics of `removeCookie(name, invalidate)`.

I think we can reduce the break even further if we do:

**HttpServerRequest.java**

```java
// original API remains
@Nullable Cookie getCookie(String name);
int cookieCount()
Map<String, Cookie> cookieMap();

// new API
@Nullable List<Cookie> getCookies(String name);
MultiMap<String, Cookie> cookies();
```

This way no code will break for users upgrading to 4.1

**HttpServerResponse.java**

```java
// original API remains
@Fluent
HttpServerResponse addCookie(Cookie cookie);

// This one must go as we can't have override of returns in java
@Nullable Cookie removeCookie(String name);
@Nullable Cookie removeCookie(String name, boolean);

// extra API
List<Cookie> removeCookie(String name);
List<Cookie> removeCookie(String name, boolean);

@Nullable Cookie removeCookie(Cookie cookie);
@Nullable Cookie removeCookie(Cookie cookie, boolean);
```

> getCookie() would just return the 1st cookie matching the given name, so the behavior would be the same as before and the cookieMap() just hides the multimap by returning again only the 1st entry, so the behavior is again the same as before.

This only holds when we use an implementation of `MultiMap` that preserves the ordering of values for a given key agreeing with the order in which the values were added (like `ArrayListMultimap`, `LinkedHashMultimap`, `LinkedListMultimap`). And we would need to return the last element to have the most recent one. Otherwise, some inconsistencies may appear:

```java
Cookie cookie = new Cookie("foo", "bar").setPath("/");
Cookie sameCookieWithDifferentValueAndPath = new Cookie("foo", "baz").setPath("/blub");

addCookie(cookie);
addCookie(sameCookieWithDifferentValue);

// the MutliMap would look like: "foo" -> [cookie, sameCookieWithDifferentValueAndPath]

System.out.println(getCookie("foo").encode());

// one with the old API would expect the output to be "{'foo': 'baz', 'path': '/blub'}"
// but it could return "{'foo': 'bar', 'path': '/'}", if the order is not respected.
```

Same applies to `cookieMap()`.

> Finally, the remove by String wipes the full matching list which again matches the old behavior and respects the contract of returning the list of matched cookies.

I agree with this one.

```java
// a simple multimap like this will preserve the insert order:
Map<String, List<Cookie>> map = new HashMap<>();
map.computeIfAbsent(l -> new ArrayList<Cookie>()).add(cookie);
```

also looking at the current implementation, in order to preserve the behavior instead of "first" element in the list we should return the "last":

```java
// CookieImpl
...
      Map<String, ServerCookie> cookies = new HashMap<>(nettyCookies.size());
      for (io.netty.handler.codec.http.cookie.Cookie cookie : nettyCookies) {
        ServerCookie ourCookie = new CookieImpl(cookie);
        cookies.put(ourCookie.getName(), ourCookie);
      }
```

Having a second look we can avoid breaking at all by doing:

**HttpServerResponse.java**

```java
// original API

@Fluent
HttpServerResponse addCookie(Cookie cookie);
// This one should be deprecated
@Deprecated @Nullable Cookie removeCookie(String name);
@Deprecated @Nullable Cookie removeCookie(String name, boolean);

// new API

// note the plural naming here to avoid conflict with the deprecated one
// and better express the API intent as the return is now a list
List<Cookie> removeCookies(String name);
List<Cookie> removeCookies(String name, boolean);

// similar to the old API but given a Cooke to remove to explicitly remove a match, not any cookie
// whose name matches
@Nullable boolean removeCookie(Cookie cookie);
@Nullable boolean removeCookie(Cookie cookie, boolean);
```

This way we avoid any breaking at the API level with existing code and allow 3 releases to users to migrate to the new `removeCookie()` for the deprecated methods.

actually shouldn't we move then to something like
```java
@Nullable boolean removeCookie(String name, String domain, String path);
@Nullable boolean removeCookie(String name, String domain, String path, boolean invalidate);
```
as knowing the maxAge, secure, httpOnly attributes is quite hard.

Just to keep it all in sync:

```java
// original API

@Fluent
HttpServerResponse addCookie(Cookie cookie);
// These ones should be deprecated
@Deprecated @Nullable Cookie removeCookie(String name);
@Deprecated @Nullable Cookie removeCookie(String name, boolean);
// this doesn't properly map all cookies
@Deprecated Map<String, Cookie> getCookies();

// new API

// alternative to `getCookies()`, Unless we also enhance Vert.x MultiMap to allow Map<String, List<Cookie>>
List<Cookies> cookies();

// note the plural naming here to avoid conflict with the deprecated one
// and better express the API intent as the return is now a list
List<Cookie> removeCookies(String name);
List<Cookie> removeCookies(String name, boolean);

// similar to the old API but given a Cookie unique tuple to explicitly remove a match, not any cookie
// that matches by name
@Nullable Cookie removeCookie(String name, String domain, String path);
@Nullable Cookie removeCookie(String name, String domain, String path, boolean invalidate);
```

Just updated the return from `boolean` to `Cookie` as we may want to work with the removed `Cookie` after removal, for example we use this during the `SessionHandler` to force a removal from the user agent.
